<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Engine wiring, the vertical slices, and the fluent builder (design 01
    section 3.1, ADR-0065's ratified name).

    THIS PROJECT TAKES ITS FRAMEWORK TYPES FROM A FrameworkReference AND NOT
    FROM PACKAGES, and that is a choice with a measured reason rather than a
    habit. `AddNamiIdentity` needs `IServiceCollection`, `IValidateOptions<T>`,
    `ValidateOnStart()` and `BindConfiguration()`. In a plain `Microsoft.NET.Sdk`
    library on net10.0 every one of those arrives as a separate NuGet package,
    which would mean four PackageVersion rows and four licence reads owed under
    ADR-0026 before a single line of Nami's own logic exists. Referencing the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework instead brings them with the runtime, which
    design 01's library table already records as ".NET and ASP.NET Core, Runtime
    and web host, MIT, ADR-0030". The engine does the same thing: read at the
    nuget.org flat container on 2026-08-08, `OpenIddict.Server.AspNetCore`
    7.5.0's nuspec declares a frameworkReferences group for net8.0, net9.0 and
    net10.0 and carries one package dependency, `OpenIddict.Server`.

    THE DEPENDENCY RULE STILL HOLDS AND IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS. Design 01
    section 3.1 says `Core` depends on "`Abstractions` plus the protocol
    engine" and "must not reference any adapter, database provider, or cloud
    SDK". A shared framework is none of those three. The architecture suite
    asserts the real rule against the compiled assembly, not against this file,
    for the reason `tests/CLAUDE.md` records: a test that reads a project file
    fails on a correct project.

    THE ENGINE REFERENCE AND ITS WIRING BOTH LANDED 2026-08-08, in seeds S-008
    and S-010. Until then this block said there was none, deliberately, and the
    sentence it ended on survived contact with two measurements: "an unused
    reference is invisible to BOTH architecture facts ... adding the reference
    with the wiring that uses it is what makes the rule able to see it". Half of
    that turned out to be true. The reference alone moved nothing, and the wiring
    put six engine assemblies into the reference table. What it did not do is make
    the namespace fact able to see a persistence call, and the comment beside the
    item group below records why.

    TargetFrameworks reads the knob rather than naming a framework, because
    ADR-0030 parameter B multi-targets libraries and single-targets the host.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Nami.Identity.Abstractions/Nami.Identity.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE ENGINE, FIVE PACKAGES OF THE EIGHT AND NOT THE OTHER THREE. Seed S-007
    settled that the engine is the eight identifiers design 04 section 6
    enumerates, which are exactly the eight `Directory.Packages.props` pins, and
    that the umbrella `OpenIddict.AspNetCore` is not one of them. Seed S-008 took
    the Server pair, and seed S-010 added the three `.Validation` packages that
    S-009's ownership table assigns here.

    No `Version` attribute, on purpose. Central Package Management fixes the
    version in `Directory.Packages.props`, and a version here is NU1008.

    WHY NOT THE OTHER THREE, each for its own reason rather than one rule.
    `.EntityFrameworkCore` is persistence and `.Quartz` is scheduling, and
    design 01 section 3.1 forbids `Core` from referencing an adapter or a
    database provider. `OpenIddict.Core` carries the managers AND the stores, and
    seed S-009 assigned its whole `.AddCore(…)` segment to the persistence
    adapter for that reason.

    THE ARCHITECTURE FACTS CHANGED STATE HERE, and not in the way the plan
    expected. Measured 2026-08-08. With a reference and no code touching it the
    assembly carried no `OpenIddict` string at all, so S-008 moved no fact. With
    the wiring, six `OpenIddict.*` entries are in the reference table. But the
    namespace fact still cannot see the violation that matters: OpenIddict
    declares every `Add*` and `Use*` extension and every builder in
    `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`, so
    `AddOpenIddict().AddCore(o => o.UseEntityFrameworkCore())` names no type
    outside the allowed list. Planting exactly that call left the namespace fact
    green, and the assembly-reference fact green too, because its forbidden
    prefixes had `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` and `Quartz` and neither matches
    an `OpenIddict.` assembly. Three prefixes were added and the plant then
    failed. `tests/Nami.Identity.ArchitectureTests/CoreDependencyRuleTests.cs`
    carries the measurement.

    WHAT ALSO CHANGED IS THE RESTORE GRAPH. Two nodes before any engine
    reference, ten after the Server pair, fourteen after the Validation three.
    `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` sections 3.4 and 3.5 carry the enumerations
    with every licence read at its own nuspec.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Server" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Server.AspNetCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Validation" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Validation.AspNetCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenIddict.Validation.ServerIntegration" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
